WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.029
Containers: Google Cloud Run as the example.

2
00:00:03.309 --> 00:00:12.888
A container platform runs your own image, so you choose the language, the libraries, and the server (the lab’s FastAPI app with uvicorn, for instance).

3
00:00:13.168 --> 00:00:17.264
Cloud Run scales containers per request, down to zero when idle.

4
00:00:17.544 --> 00:00:32.179
Its request limit is a request timeout: Cloud Run’s docs give a default of 300 seconds, configurable up to 3,600 seconds (60 minutes), with a 504 when a service doesn’t respond within the limit.

5
00:00:32.459 --> 00:00:44.128
The page describes the timeout as the time a service has to return a response and does not single out streaming, so measure a long stream against your configured timeout before you rely on it.

6
00:00:44.408 --> 00:00:58.467
Containers fit when you need control: a Python service with dependencies that won’t fit a function, long agent runs, a worker process that drains a queue, or the same image running locally, in CI, and in production.

7
00:00:58.747 --> 00:01:04.827
The trade is more to own: the image, its base-layer patches, its health checks, its concurrency settings.
